# Conversion lift study: Always-on prospecting, September 2026

Prepared by the paid social agency for Ridgeline Running. Study run on Meta, 1 to 28 September 2026.

## Design

- Audience split at random: 70% test (saw our ads), 30% control (saw a public service ad, PSA, in the same placements).
- Conversion = online purchase within the study window, any device.
- Weekly frequency cap during the study: 6 impressions per user per week.

## Headline

| Cell | Users | Converters | Conversion rate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Test | 209,874 | 2,788 | 1.33% |
| Control | 90,126 | 1,056 | 1.17% |

Lift: **+13.4%** (p = 0.0005). Split check: 70.0% / 30.0% against the planned 70 / 30, fine.

Incremental conversions = lift x control conversions = 0.134 x 1,056 = **141**.
Media spend on the test cell: EUR 9,301 (the PSA impressions, EUR 4,010, are a one-off study cost and are left out).
Incremental CPA = EUR 66; at EUR 41 contribution per order, **POAS = -0.38**. The campaign loses money as it runs today.

## Segments (lift_by_segment.csv)

- Device: mobile lift 18.0%, desktop 6.2%. Desktop is barely moving.
- Age: 65+ shows the strongest lift of any group, 83%. 18-24 shows -5.2%; with these numbers that is within noise, so no change for 18-24.
- Exposure: users who saw 10 or more ads converted at 2.27%, against 0.78% for 1-2 ads. More exposure clearly drives purchase.

## Recommendations

1. Cut the total budget by 25% until POAS is positive.
2. Within what is left, stop desktop delivery and move it to mobile.
3. Add a 65+ ad set with its own budget.
4. Raise the weekly frequency cap from 6 to 10.
DEVILS_ANALYST_EOF
